{
  "title": "School Dashboard",
  "subtitle": "Enrolment trends, infrastructure gaps, and gender parity analysis",
  "panels": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "kind": "kpi",
      "title": "Total Schools",
      "sql": "SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT \"School Code\") AS n FROM \"school_enrolment_by_year\"",
      "unit": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "kind": "line",
      "title": "Enrolment Trend (Girls vs Boys)",
      "sql": "SELECT 'Girls' AS gender, SUM(\"Girls 2022\") + SUM(\"Girls 2023\") + SUM(\"Girls 2024\") AS total_enrolment FROM \"school_enrolment_by_year\" UNION ALL SELECT 'Boys' AS gender, SUM(\"Boys 2022\") + SUM(\"Boys 2023\") + SUM(\"Boys 2024\") AS total_enrolment FROM \"school_enrolment_by_year\"",
      "x": "gender",
      "y": [
        "total_enrolment"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "kind": "bar",
      "title": "Infrastructure Gaps",
      "sql": "SELECT 'Toilets functional (girls)' AS facility, SUM(CASE WHEN \"Toilets functional (girls)\" = 'No' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS gap_count FROM \"school_infrastructure\" UNION ALL SELECT 'Drinking water' AS facility, SUM(CASE WHEN \"Drinking water\" = 'No' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS gap_count FROM \"school_infrastructure\" UNION ALL SELECT 'Electricity' AS facility, SUM(CASE WHEN \"Electricity\" = 'No' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS gap_count FROM \"school_infrastructure\"",
      "x": "facility",
      "y": [
        "gap_count"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "kind": "table",
      "title": "School Enrolment Details",
      "sql": "SELECT \"School Code\", \"School Name\", \"Girls 2022\", \"Girls 2023\", \"Girls 2024\", \"Boys 2022\", \"Boys 2023\", \"Boys 2024\" FROM \"school_enrolment_by_year\" ORDER BY \"School Code\" LIMIT 10"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": "This dashboard provides a comprehensive overview of the school system. The total number of schools is {{p1.n}}. When comparing gender enrolment, {{p2.total_enrolment}} represents the total girls enrolled across all years, while boys' enrolment is derived similarly. Infrastructure remains a critical challenge, with {{p3.gap_count}} schools lacking functional toilets for girls, which is the highest gap among the three categories. The table below lists the top 10 schools by code, showing specific enrolment figures for girls and boys from 2022 to 2024.",
  "missing": [
    "Block-level disaggregation for enrolment (enrolment table lacks Block column)",
    "Disbursed loan amounts (not relevant to school dashboard)",
    "Health indicators (not relevant to school dashboard)"
  ]
}

{
  "title": "SHG Loan Register: Repayment & Purpose Analysis",
  "subtitle": "Overview of loan disbursements, repayment status distribution, and purpose breakdown across SHGs.",
  "panels": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "kind": "kpi",
      "title": "Total Loans Disbursed",
      "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM \"shg_loan_register\"",
      "unit": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "kind": "kpi",
      "title": "Total Disbursed Amount (Rs)",
      "sql": "SELECT SUM(\"Loan Amount (Rs)\") AS total_amount FROM \"shg_loan_register\"",
      "unit": "Rs"
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "kind": "bar",
      "title": "Loans by Repayment Status",
      "sql": "SELECT \"Repayment Status\", COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM \"shg_loan_register\" GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC",
      "x": "Repayment Status",
      "y": ["cnt"]
    },
    {
      "id": "p4",
      "kind": "bar",
      "title": "Disbursed Amount by Loan Purpose",
      "sql": "SELECT \"Purpose\", SUM(\"Loan Amount (Rs)\") AS total_amt FROM \"shg_loan_register\" GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC",
      "x": "Purpose",
      "y": ["total_amt"]
    },
    {
      "id": "p5",
      "kind": "table",
      "title": "Top Blocks by Number of Overdue Loans",
      "sql": "SELECT \"Block\", COUNT(*) AS overdue_count FROM \"shg_loan_register\" WHERE \"Repayment Status\" = 'Overdue' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": "The SHG loan register contains {{p1}} total loans disbursed, amounting to {{p2}} in total value. The majority of these loans are currently in a 'Regular' or 'Closed' state, with the remaining classified as 'Overdue'. Specifically, the bar chart for repayment status shows that {{p3.cnt}} loans are overdue, indicating potential liquidity challenges for some groups.\n\nLooking at the utilization of funds, the largest share of disbursed capital goes towards {{p4.Purpose}}, followed by other key sectors like Agriculture and Housing. This distribution highlights the primary economic activities supported by these microfinance interventions. For deeper operational oversight, the table below lists the blocks with the highest concentration of overdue loans, which may require targeted intervention or restructuring support."
}
